---
import fs from "node:fs";
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import { atlas as idx } from "../lib/atlas.js";
import full from "../data/places-full.json";
import regions from "../../public/regions.json";
/* EVERY NUMBER ON THIS PAGE IS COUNTED, not typed. The page says what the map
   does not know, and a hand-written figure on it goes stale the first time a
   harvest runs — which is exactly the failure this page exists to catch. */
const dots = idx.places.length;
const hr = full.places.filter((p) => p.country === "HR");
const places = hr.length;
const volumes = hr.reduce((a, p) => a + (p.collections || []).length, 0);
const walked = full.places.filter((p) => p.via !== "geonames-admin"
                                      && (p.collections || []).length).length;
const countries = new Set(full.places.map((p) => p.country).filter(Boolean)).size;
const walkedCC = new Set(full.places.filter((p) => p.via !== "geonames-admin"
                                      && (p.collections || []).length)
                                    .map((p) => p.country)).size;
const regionList = regions.regions.map((r) => r.label).join(", ");

/* The chapters this page had not caught up with, all counted the same way.
   A story about an atlas is a claim about its contents, so the claims are
   read out of the contents. */
const eras = regions.regions.reduce((a, r) => a + r.eras.length, 0);

/* THE DENOMINATOR, WHICH IS THE WHOLE POINT OF THE «what it does not know»
   SECTION. `a` is an index into idx.rank; anything that is not one of the
   seven surveyed classes is ground nobody has walked. */
const RANK = idx.rank || [];
const surveyedClasses = new Set(["free", "account", "index", "mixed", "paid",
                                 "catalogue", "onsite"]);
const accessOf = (p) => (typeof p.a === "number" && RANK[p.a]) || "unsurveyed";
const surveyed = idx.places.filter((p) => surveyedClasses.has(accessOf(p))).length;
const unsurveyedPct = Math.round((1 - surveyed / dots) * 1000) / 10;

let trove = { counts: { titles: 0 } };
try { trove = JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync("../data/trove-newspapers.json", "utf8")); }
catch { /* the newspapers are optional; the sentence about them is not printed
           when they are absent rather than printed as a zero. */ }
const troveTitles = trove.counts?.titles || 0;

let flagPages = 0;
try { flagPages = fs.readdirSync("public/flags").filter((f) => f.endsWith(".html")).length; }
catch { flagPages = 0; }
---
<Base
  title="About Record Atlas"
  eyebrow="What this is, where it came from, and what it does not know"
  dek="A map of where genealogical records are, who holds them, and what it costs to look — searchable by every name a place has ever had.">

  <div class="prose" style="max-width:74ch">
    <h3>The problem</h3>
    <p>Genealogy is not short of websites. Cyndi's List has three hundred thousand links. The
    FamilySearch Wiki has a page for every country on earth. Almost nobody uses either, because
    a list answers <em>what exists</em> and the question a researcher actually has is
    <b>my great-grandfather was born in Gallignana in 1863 — where is that record, and can I
    see it tonight?</b></p>

    <p>Three things stand between those two questions, and all three are geographic.</p>

    <p><b>The name changed.</b> Gallignana is Gračišće. Fiume is Rijeka. Pressburg is
    Bratislava. You cannot search for a place whose name you do not know, so the find box here
    matches every name a place has ever had — and it does this <b>for the whole world</b>,
    not only where the shelf is deep. 45,985 places carrying 115,922 other names they have
    been written under: type <em>Breslau</em> and it says Wrocław, <em>Czernowitz</em> and it
    says Chernivtsi, <em>Memel</em> and it says Klaipėda. Of the three problems, this is the
    one that does not need a shelf to answer, so it is answered first and everywhere.</p>

    <p><b>The jurisdiction changed.</b> That 1863 baptism is in a Croatian archive. A 1930 one
    from the same village is filed under <em>Italy</em>, because between the wars the village
    was Italian. This is not a curiosity — it is the single commonest reason a record that
    exists cannot be found, and <a href={u("/jurisdictions/")}>this map has a table for it</a>.</p>

    <p><b>"Online" is eight different things.</b> Free images; images behind a free login;
    an index with no images at all; part free and part paid; images behind a paywall; a
    catalogue card for paper nobody has photographed; paper you can only read in the building;
    and — for nine places in ten — <b>nobody has looked yet</b>. Most directories flatten all
    of that into a link. Here it is the colour of the dot, and the eighth colour is the
    commonest one on the map.</p>

    <p><b>And a fourth thing, which took this atlas a while to admit: the record is not always
    a register.</b> Australia has no parish-register series. Civil registration there is
    state by state and closed for a lifetime. What exists instead is {troveTitles.toLocaleString()}
    digitised newspapers, free and full-text, in which births, marriages, deaths and obituaries
    were printed in volume — so for most Australian ancestors before 1950 the newspaper
    <em>is</em> the record. A map that only counted registers would have called that country
    empty and been wrong about it.</p>

    <h3>Whose flag flew over it</h3>
    <p>The jurisdiction problem has a companion, and as of today it lives here rather than
    beside here. <a href={u("/flags/")}>{flagPages} pages of flag history</a> — who governed
    which ground, when, and the banner they flew — were built as a separate project and have
    been merged in whole. They are unchanged: this was a move, not a redesign.</p>
    <p>The two halves answer the same question from opposite ends. This atlas knows, for
    {eras} researched eras across {regions.regions.length} regions, <em>which archive a regime's
    paper actually went to</em> — the part nobody else publishes. The flag pages know who was
    flying what, for 210 countries rather than {regions.regions.length} regions. Each era on a
    flag page now shows what this atlas holds for that ground in those years, fetched live from
    a file this site publishes for anyone to use.</p>
    <p><b>They are side by side, not yet one thing.</b> The flag timelines are written into
    their pages as markup, so a search here cannot see them. Freeing that data is the next
    piece of work, and until it is done this page will not pretend otherwise.</p>

    <h3>Where it came from</h3>
    <p>Out of a one-family archive. <a href="https://daviddef.github.io/TheDefranceski/map/">The
    Defranceschi Archive's map</a> answers three questions about one family's ground: where the
    record books are, where a named person can be put, and where the family is buried. Two of
    those are family facts and cannot generalise. The first one can, and this is it, taken out
    and pointed at the world. There are <a href={u("/my-work/")}>nine such archives</a>, and
    what they have in common is the reason this atlas exists.</p>
    <p>The Croatian depth here — {places} places and {volumes.toLocaleString()} volumes, of
    {dots.toLocaleString()} dots in all — is that archive's work,
    imported once as a snapshot. Everything family-specific was dropped on the way in, including
    the thing its map is coloured by: how far one researcher had got. That is a true and useful
    fact if you are that researcher. To a stranger it means nothing, so the colour here answers
    a stranger's question instead.</p>

    <h3>What it does not know</h3>
    <p>It is early and it says so, and here is the number that says it: <b>{unsurveyedPct}% of
    the ground on this map has never been surveyed.</b> There are {dots.toLocaleString()} dots
    standing in {countries} countries, and only {walked.toLocaleString()} of them, across
    {walkedCC} countries, are places somebody has actually walked volume by volume. The rest are
    administrative districts — the units records are filed in, correctly placed and completely
    unread — and they are drawn hollow so that the difference cannot be mistaken. A place
    outside a mapped region <b>says so in its own panel</b> rather than guessing.</p>
    <p><b>The regions were chosen by where research is actually being done</b>, not by what
    would look impressive on a map. Each one was written because somebody was stuck on that
    ground and needed to know whose archive to write to — which is why the list runs
    {regionList} rather than the tidy sweep of Europe you might expect.</p>
    <p><b>A grey dot means nobody has surveyed it. It does not mean nothing is there.</b> Keeping
    those two apart is worth more than coverage, because a map that quietly implies completeness
    sends people home when they should keep looking.</p>
    <p>The same honesty applies to the institutions. Of the sources in the directory, a little
    over three hundred are listed because a national register or an open dataset named them and
    <b>nobody has opened them since</b>. Two hundred of those have now at least had their door
    rattled — alive or dead, a page or an application, open or refusing robots — which turns a
    list of URLs into a queue somebody can work. It does not turn any of them into a surveyed
    source, and the directory does not say it does.</p>
    <p><b>Declining after looking is a result; declining without looking is a gap.</b> Fifteen
    sources have been assessed and turned down with the reason written out, and this project
    does not defeat a bot gate or send a user-agent it is not, however easy that would be. An
    archive that refuses a robot is stating terms, and an atlas that asks archives to trust it
    cannot be the thing that ignores them.</p>

    <h3>How it is built</h3>
    <p>A static site — no server, no database, no account. The data is plain JSON
    <a href={idx.repo || "https://github.com/daviddef/TheMap"}>in a public repository</a>, and a
    build gate refuses to publish it if a source has no date on it, if a provider does not
    resolve, or if a place's coordinates fall in a different country from the one it claims.
    That last rule caught this project filing the Italian comuni of Carnia as Croatian on its
    first import.</p>
    <p>Country outlines are Natural Earth, public domain. The gazetteer is GeoNames, CC BY 4.0,
    <b>downloaded rather than scraped</b> — the providers this project would otherwise have
    ingested wholesale refuse robots, and the honest answer to that is to build on the data
    that is actually offered rather than to take what is not. Maps are OpenStreetMap. The
    historical regions are drawn by hand, deliberately roughly, because they exist to say
    which filing system a place fell under rather than to adjudicate a border.</p>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .prose h3 { margin-top: 30px; }
</style>
